Highball: msync, a multi-object wait that returns early takes its registration back (highball#224)

msync_wait_multiple() registers the thread with the wineserver's msync thread for every object,
then spins until the server has answered. If an object becomes available during the spin, it
returned STATUS_PENDING after lowering the waiter counts, without telling the server. The server
still processed the registration and, when nothing was signaled by then, kept the thread on each
object's list. Nothing took it off again: the lowered counts mean a later signal does not message
the server either, so an object that is never signaled (a quit event, say) grows by one node per
lost race. On the server side, a registration that met a signaled object at index i unregistered
objects 0..i-1 only when i > 1, so a wait on {quit, work} with work signaled leaked quit's node
every time. After 524288 nodes the pool printed "msync: warn: node memory pool exhausted" on every
further allocation and fell back to malloc, and the wineserver grew until the Mac ran out of memory.

- client: the early return goes through server_remove_wait() like every other exit, which also
  lowers the alert object's count that the old loop left raised.
- server: unregister objects 0..i-1 whenever i > 0.
- server: the pool warning prints once.

Reproduced 2026-09-30 on an M4, macOS 27.0, with msyncleak.exe (a worker sets an auto-reset event
in a loop while the main thread waits on {quit, work}): the pool runs out within seconds and the
wineserver's RSS grows from 30 MB to 77 MB in 30 s on x64-crossover26.3-r12 (33 MB to 87 MB on
Highball's Wine 10 engine, x64-sikarugir10.0_6-r15, same symptom). My Summer Car (highball#224)
fills its log with the same warning over a long session.

Upstream: msync is CrossOver's (and Sikarugir's) implementation, not WineHQ's.

